## Advisory items

1. `publish.yaml` for h7-bulb-storage carries an uncommitted operator edit to `manufacturing`. Step 4 must delete only the `images: []` line there and must not revert, restage wholesale, or reformat that file. Stage that file with `git add -p` (or leave the commit of that file to the operator) so the operator's prose does not ride in this task's commit under the wrong message. The same file pair also drives release-please: a `docs` or `fix` commit on a model directory proposes a patch release for that model, so use `chore` for the `images` removal if no release is wanted for it.
2. Call `version_from_tag` and `changelog_url` with the other argument checks at the top of `publish` (before `_mutation_arguments` and any network call), so a bad tag refuses without a request. The plan does not fix the order.
3. The operator's example deep link `{changelog_url}#{version}` will not land on a heading: release-please writes headings like `## [1.0.2](compare-url) (date)`, whose GitHub anchor is not `#1.0.2`. The plan correctly adds no fragment itself; the operator may want to know their own `#{version}` text will not scroll.
4. Consider recording the three tokens in memory-bank/systemPatterns.md (which fields they apply to, and that `name` is deliberately excluded because a later publish finds the listing by it). The plan edits that file only to drop `images`; the name exclusion is a contract an unrelated contributor could break.

## Radical innovation (advisory)

Replace the ordered `str.replace` chain with one single-pass renderer: `render_listing_text(text, context)` using `re.sub(r"\{(version|changelog_url|license_name)\}", lambda m: context[m.group(1)], text)`, where `context` is built once in `publish` and passed to `assemble_description` and the manufacturing argument instead of the two keyword arguments plus `license_name`. A single pass removes the order dependence that the plan lists as its first Challenge (a substituted `changelog_url` can never be re-scanned), shrinks `assemble_description`'s signature, and gives one place to refuse or warn on a token that is close to a real one but not equal to it (for example `{verison}`), which would otherwise ship to Cults as literal text. Cost: it changes the planned `fill_listing_text` signature and step 2/3 test stubs, so weigh it against redesigning an already-planned unit.
